{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q12",
  "speechText": "For me, I would start with the user goal and keep the agent inside a clear workflow. The main reason is that I would not send the whole database to the model. I would only retrieve the relevant and authorized context for that user. For example, if a user asks about training progress, the agent may need the user profile, recent workouts, goals, and progress metrics. Because of that, I would expose a small set of backend tools. The model can decide which tool it needs, but the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, checks permission, executes the action, and returns a structured result. This is similar to what I worked with in Clincove, where the agent could search and read documents through controlled backend tools, while the backend kept control of the access scope and permissions. At the same time, I would track run IDs, tool calls, latency, and errors, and use retry and recovery where needed. I would also add regression and eval cases for important workflows. So overall, the model can make decisions, but the backend stays in control of data, permissions, execution, and reliability.",
